  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote `src/main.ts`, which passes `typecheck`. I couldn't run a browser or `vite build` here, so none of it has been looked at on screen.\n\n- **Island:** a 38×38 jittered heightfield with a wobbly radial outline, built as separate triangles with per-face normals and per-triangle colours so it reads as flat-shaded. The colour bands are sea floor, sand, grass, rock and summit. About 24 trees (a trunk plus two cones each) and 14 rocks (low-segment spheres) sit on it, placed by terrain height.\n- **Water:** a 320 m custom-shader plane with three summed sine waves in the vertex stage and facet normals from screen-space derivatives. It shades from turquoise near the island to deep blue, with Fresnel mixing into the horizon colour, a sun or moon glint, and a distance fade. The uniforms follow the time of day.\n- **Day/night:** `sky.preetham` with stars. The sun and sky update every frame, and one cycle takes 20 s. The sun light's colour and intensity follow its elevation, and a moon light and a hemisphere light carry the night. A faint constant ambient light keeps the scene from going black.\n- **Camera:** it sits 10.5 m above the water at 38 m from the island, with fov 55. That framing should leave the whole island and a strip of sky in view. It orbits slowly through `app.camera.setPose`.\n- **Ready flag:** `window.__EVAL_READY__` is set after `app.ready()` and two animation frames.\n\nTwo details are unconfirmed:\n- The water shader declares its colour and light uniforms as `vec3` and updates them through `setUniform`. I confirmed only that `f32` uniforms work this way from the docs recipe.\n- The water's horizon colour is an estimate and may not match the sky exactly at the water's far edge.\n\n",
